Is it standard practice for @Vivo_India & @iQOOInd authorized service centers to withhold a repaired handset until an ordinary customer signs away their fundamental legal rights?

I am a student from Jammu & Kashmir. I had to travel nearly 180 km to visit the authorized service center. When the repair was completed and I was about to receive my handset, the staff explicitly told me that signing this pre-printed letter is 100% compulsory — without this signature, they refused to hand over my own device.

Being a student with ongoing academic exams, no secondary phone, and running late to travel 180 km back home, I had zero practical choice or bargaining power on the spot. To get my device released, I complied and signed the document.

Take a hard look at what this compulsory undertaking makes a consumer accept:

1. "No defect or inherent issue was found... functioning as per specifications"
Yet the very next sentence confirms the company replaced the entire motherboard. If no defect was found, why was the core motherboard swapped?

2. Blanket Waiver of Consumer Rights:
"I also confirm that I will not pursue any further legal action or initiate any complaint against the Company, vivo Mobile India Pvt. Ltd... before any court, forum, tribunal, or any other authority."

3. Complete Discharge of Liability:
"I further undertake to treat the matter as fully and finally resolved and hereby release and discharge the Company... from any and all present or future claims, demands, or liabilities related to the subject handset."
They literally demand an unconditional exit from all post-repair responsibility.

4. Mandatory Aadhar Card & Personal Details:
Compulsorily demanding government ID details as a prerequisite to hand over a customer's personal property.

The Legal Reality:

* Section 28 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872: Any agreement restraining an individual from enforcing their rights via ordinary legal tribunals/courts is void ab initio.
* Consumer Protection Act, 2019: Making the return of a customer’s property conditional upon signing liability waivers amounts to an Unfair Trade Practice.

Is making such indemnity undertakings compulsory an official corporate protocol, @Vivo_India @iQOOInd?

